Ruby 4.0's shape tree's memory in WebAssembly, for the plugin's ruby.wasm, which scripts/ruby_wasm.rb builds with this.

Ruby reserves its shape tree's buffers, 280 MB, with mmap as it starts. Natively that's free, as the OS only backs the
pages it touches, but wasi's emulated mmap allocates and zeroes all of it, taking 150 to 200 ms each time Ruby starts.
So in shape.c they're fresh WebAssembly memory, from memory.grow, which is already zeroed and only backed by the OS as
it's touched, as mmap's is natively. They're never unmapped, so the memory's never given back.

It's inside #if defined(__wasi__) && defined(HAVE_MMAP), beside Ruby's own code, which is left as it is.

To change it, edit Ruby's source in build/ruby_wasm/build/checkouts/4.0, then diff it against its tarball's.

--- a/shape.c
+++ b/shape.c
@@ -21,6 +21,22 @@
 #endif
 
 #define REDBLACK_CACHE_SIZE (SHAPE_BUFFER_SIZE * 32)
+
+#if defined(__wasi__) && defined(HAVE_MMAP)
+/*
+  The shape tree's buffers, 280 MB, from fresh WebAssembly memory, which is zeroed, and only backed by the OS as it's
+  touched, as mmap's is natively, rather than from wasi's emulated mmap, which allocates and zeroes it all at each start.
+  They're never unmapped.
+*/
+static void *
+shape_buffer_alloc(size_t size)
+{
+    size_t at = __builtin_wasm_memory_grow(0, (size + 65535) / 65536);
+    if (at == (size_t)-1) return MAP_FAILED;
+    return (void *)(at * 65536);
+}
+# define mmap(addr, size, prot, flags, fd, offset) shape_buffer_alloc(size)
+#endif
 
 /* This depends on that the allocated memory by Ruby's allocator or
  * mmap is not located at an odd address. */
